WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.980
Shadowgate, your next move could be your last.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:27.240
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.240
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:42.340
And I'm Joe. And we are playing today what could be seen as the precursor to all current Escape the Room style experiences across the world, right?

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:46.280
This is kind of an Escape the Room, except for you've chosen to go in the room.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.760
I mean, you could call it that.

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:52.340
I'd also just say it's a like a point and click adventure game.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:59.600
Yeah, I feel like Escape the Rooms have always sort of reminded me of this format of game, but it wasn't until

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:06.540
you know, later in my experience of playing this game, I actually did start thinking specifically this feels like Escape the Room.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.440
So I don't know. It's like one of those chicken and egg things like something came.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:11.520
They're both kind of evoking each other.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.260
And Sean, you're spot on.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:15.520
It is absolutely a point and click adventure game.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:40.880
I only noticed as I was getting like further into the game that it just became a lot more about my inventory and less about like what was going on in the actual rooms that that made me start to feel like an Escape the Room where I have to deal with the tools that I've collected less, you know, less pointing and clicking than I'm used to in these kinds of games where like, okay, it doesn't matter what you have available to you.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:46.740
It's what's on the screen that you're like trying to puzzle solve based on that, not based on what you've collected so far.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:58.820
Yeah, I guess there is enough of a difference between the two types, but I guess I would consider Escape the Room more a subgenre of point and click adventure.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:10.280
Yeah, I guess Escape the Room, I would say is like just solely focused on like puzzle solving where this is like there's a lot of puzzle solving, but there's also just like a lot of general adventuring going on.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:17.500
And there's a lot of story too, not even just like what you read in the manual and stuff like that, what we're used to in games.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:23.140
There's a lot of dialogue to or exposition or narrative to read throughout the game.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:28.960
And then to also look at all the objects in the room and get detailed descriptions on that stuff.

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:32.600
This is a very text heavy game compared to what we're used to on the system.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:54.980
I think that a lot of the backstory, it's a little opaque and in some of it, like because there is definitely a text limit, it doesn't, I couldn't entirely get what all of the, like the circle of 12 and the something of seven, like I didn't really get what all that was.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:57.520
But you're right, I mean there is a lot of that.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:09.520
Yeah, I also think that it being text based primarily, I mean obviously there's images too, but it's just normally stills with like very few animations and stuff.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:21.740
Kind of, while like the text is obviously limited, it kind of helps to like remove limits in the video game sense of as far as like letting them do a lot of different things.

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:24.660
Because when it all boils down, all they have to do is text.

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:44.480
Yeah. Like in that kind of, I think, whether artificially or not, like I think it makes, at least while I'm playing it, it makes me be able to kind of like immerse myself and like kind of make it, it feels like a much more robust game than other like games in this like fantasy genre.

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:47.260
Just because like, it's almost like reading a book.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:53.800
Yeah, and I think that becomes the whole like appeal of Shadowgate 30 years later, right?

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:59.120
What I'm saying is that in other fantasy genre games, you know, you could use like Final Fantasy as an example here.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:13.120
You control this group of characters on their epic adventure and you directly interact as that character in the overworld doing these things, saying I, you know, like attacking and being tactical.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:23.520
In this game, you are a character and you are exploring this, you know, castle throughout first person view.

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:33.980
But the way you interact with this kind of video game is way different than, you know, it's gonna sound silly, but like any of the platformers we've played or anything else.

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:41.400
Like you're not, you just have like the shield on you and provided that you have the shield equipped, the game does all the work for you.

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:46.400
There's no like, okay, now we're switching to a combat mode where you fight the dragon, right?

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:48.360
It doesn't, the game never takes that way.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:57.040
So in that way, Joe, I totally understand what you're saying, where it does become more of a book where you're collecting these things or choosing.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:58.760
It's like a choose your own adventure book.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:10.400
You're choosing to perform an action and then based on where you are, what you have, what you've done, the game kind of like reads the following paragraph to you.

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:14.680
And then you choose the next part of the adventure based off of that information.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:20.340
Yeah, you're not like, you don't have skills in this game.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:26.100
Like you don't, if you, in the game, you are like an adventurer night guy.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:32.680
So it assumes that if you have the correct equipment, you're probably going to do pretty well.

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:36.200
Otherwise, it would be kind of weird and pointless.

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:47.420
Right. If it relied on reflexes or fast input from the players at any point, that could get a little weird because of the way that the game works.

00:05:47.420 --> 00:06:00.440
So I feel like before we get any further, similar to what Sean and I did in our Nostalgia Bites episode, the Portopia serial murder case, we should explain or, you know, just talk a little bit about all the different things that you're allowed to do in this game.

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:06.680
And as a brief overview of that, you're allowed to move where you select in a little mini map.

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:11.400
It shows you open areas via square boxes, and then you can click on those square boxes.

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:16.580
Or if you see said door on the actual screen, you can also move into those.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:21.560
You can look, which allows you to get a better description of specific things in the room.

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:23.580
So you might be able to tell it's a sword.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:30.920
But if you look at the sword, it would give you a more detailed explanation of what that sword could possibly be used for later on in the game.

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:37.420
Or it could be a very, very vague hint that you wouldn't be able to understand without the accompanying strategy guide.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:49.620
There's the use function, which allows you to use an item, usually a combination where you select the item first and then what you want to use it on or use it on yourself.

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:53.880
There's open and close, which is as straightforward as it sounds.

00:06:53.980 --> 00:07:00.280
You can open up a door or a chest or anything else that you think could open and then close it as well if need be.

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:05.040
You can take anything that you can see and you can hit anything that you want.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:12.400
Not saying that it will actually let you take those things or that hitting those things will do anything, but the option is available to every single object in the game.

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:16.160
You can speak by talking out.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:25.740
You can control yourself by the self option, which allows you to use items on yourself or do things in the room with yourself.

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:28.800
And then additional things you can save.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:31.640
You can move between the inventory and you can see all your goods.

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:38.540
Those last three are really more of like inventory and game management things, not necessarily related to the actual world of Shadowgate.

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:48.760
But I think now that we've laid out those moves, that's kind of that shows, I think, how many permutations in any given room of things that you have available to you.

00:07:48.760 --> 00:08:03.400
And what makes this game so broad and branching from the player's standpoint is that every single room you enter, you could just try to brute force your way into another room or into collecting everything in the room.

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:06.040
But that's not the best way to play Shadowgate.

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:13.660
And you kind of need to understand everything you can do here and then use those tools to explore said room.

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:21.680
Yeah, and that being said, I feel like they do a really good job of because you're thrown into this game with all of these options.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:25.220
And it's almost a little overwhelming at first because you're just like all these things you can do.

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:34.940
And I think there's just some really good game design in just the first couple of rooms that that gives you that teaches you what to do and when to do it.

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:41.740
And like the kind of things you should look out for in this game, like in the very first two rooms, you know, you get to the second room.

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:43.940
There's already a dead end. There's a locked door without a key.

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:50.320
And outside of it, you have a door that you would have presumably already used the open command on.

00:08:50.600 --> 00:09:02.920
And if you start looking at things, you'll find like there's there's a skull, a little skull above the door that says like, oh, this really makes you think that like that like danger lurks inside or something.

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:07.480
It uses the word lurks inside, making you think like, you know, there's nothing else you can do here.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:16.380
So you might just start trying to like use the open command on the on the skull, maybe because it said something lurks inside when you clicked on the skull, too.

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:27.860
So it's like giving you that like a little bit of a little bit of a like a lob ball of a puzzle just so you can like understand like, oh, OK, not everything is necessarily exactly as it seems.

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:28.880
And I should try things.

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:37.600
And then following that, you go into the next room and right away, there's a book that you're just like, I'm going to take this book and you take the book and you die.

00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:41.820
Like most people, I assume, just like they see a book and like, I'll take it, see what it is.

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:51.820
But if you would, if you examine it first, it kind of gives you this like idea, like maybe I should be careful, you know, so like it kind of gives you the idea, like examine things.

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:56.580
They'll give you hints and like try different things like that you wouldn't expect.

00:09:56.660 --> 00:09:59.520
Right off the bat, you kind of know, like that's the kind of game you're looking for.

00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:01.920
It's not going to be necessarily like telegraph for you.

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:03.160
You're going to have to figure things out.

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:24.640
Yeah. And stepping back a bit, just to talk about the actions that you can take, it is a very wide like palette of interactability when it comes to like what you can, how you're interacting with the world, what options you have.

00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:40.880
Um, and going back to like comparison to that, to Portopia, uh, which was a lot more focused on like mystery solving and the things that you could do were very much about solving, uh, murder cases.

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:51.160
Uh, this one is much more just like, what can you tactic or tactilely with your hands do, uh, in the space provided?

00:10:51.160 --> 00:11:00.380
Uh, but going off of Joe's point, yes, I think that those are really cool, uh, tutorials in, in those first couple areas.

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:03.780
I, for one, I felt really dumb.

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:12.240
Uh, I actually did have to open up the guide immediately because I couldn't understand that opening a skull was a thing.

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:16.740
Um, and, uh, but I definitely died when I took the book.

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:22.960
Like, so that, that taught me that anything could be a trap and I should be wary of these things.

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:36.280
Uh, but the skull thing was more of a, like, well, okay, this game is starting me off on the wrong foot because it's immediately going to, to something kind of out there.

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:49.180
I thought maybe it could, could have started with something a little bit more, a little less, um, lateral, lateral thinking and more so just like, okay, this is how you interact with something.

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:59.380
Yeah. I, I think it's because, uh, in Legend of Zelda, right, the tutorial there is to enter that cave that is very clearly seen to the person.

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:07.700
You can go walk off without a sword and try to get as far as you can and you'll have nothing to fight back with, but you enter the cave and then you get the sword.

00:12:08.020 --> 00:12:11.300
That's, that's somewhat of, of a very beginning tutorial.

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:19.640
There's the Super Mario Brothers World 1-1 where obviously we've talked about it at length in that episode, but it teaches you the idea of jumping over pitfalls.

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:28.740
This game probably has to be a little more difficult just to, you know, set up the idea of what you're, how you're going to be solving puzzles in this game.

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:31.620
It's not like the world's hardest puzzle or anything.

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:40.500
Uh, I think Joe saying that, you know, by using the look function, it would give you a little more detail about that skull and you'd be able to theoretically solve it on your own.

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:41.800
I'm not saying you're dumb, Sean.

00:12:41.940 --> 00:12:42.980
I didn't solve it either.

00:12:43.340 --> 00:12:47.240
Uh, but it's not like, it's not the world's worst tutorial either.

00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:56.500
It's not like they purposely made it hard, but yes, they totally could have had something that was more chest-like that you then had to open to find the key that you needed.

00:12:56.660 --> 00:12:58.400
And then you understand like, oh yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:00.340
So I got to open things to get items.

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:05.060
I got to take said item and then I have to use it on the door in the same room.

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:13.460
This one's a little interesting because you actually have to like exit back out of the castle and go use the only other thing in your surroundings.

00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:20.400
Uh, but I, I do think it's a great puzzle now that I've played most of Shadowgate.

00:13:20.540 --> 00:13:37.860
Like I do think it has a great way of explaining to you that this game is going to have some, uh, really, uh, little hidden trinkets all around that you're going to have to kind of explore the pixel art and say like, hmm, maybe this isn't just on the wall.

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:40.720
And maybe this is something that I need to interact with.

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:44.000
You know, we've seen that a few times in this game, even bricks.

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:46.940
It's like, hmm, that brick is slightly off colored.

00:13:46.980 --> 00:13:47.860
I should hit it.

00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:59.920
You know, you can definitely tell that like this game, it, it, it lends itself well to sort of like playing as a team with like, Ooh, why don't you try using that on that?

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:01.600
Or like, well, that thing looks weird.

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:03.380
Let's, why don't you try doing that?

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:21.260
Um, but the game definitely expects you to get stuck a lot because I feel like the only way that somebody would decide to try and open a slightly brighter brick than something else is if they've literally tried every other option.

00:14:22.360 --> 00:14:31.120
Well, I'll say too, that I, I may have been, um, been a little generous when I said like, Oh, the hint of like something lurks inside, uh, is a great hint.

00:14:31.160 --> 00:14:33.020
I mean, you know, maybe that that's still kind of a tough hint.

00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:38.660
I guess I kind of like lucked into this, but, but like, I just went into the second room.

00:14:38.920 --> 00:14:44.760
There was no key there. I went back out and I just started trying things and I was like, well, there's only a few things on the screen, so I'll try this skull.

00:14:44.920 --> 00:14:47.980
I did try to take the skull, but that wasn't it.

00:14:47.980 --> 00:14:58.500
Yeah. Cause I feel like if you just imagine a world where the guide is not that accessible to us, I feel like just with time, like there's only so many things you could try.

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:12.700
And so like, it's not that that puzzle in itself is not like a game breakingly frustrating puzzle where like, if you just didn't have the option to, to look at the manual, you probably would have found it eventually just by trial and error.

00:15:12.700 --> 00:15:16.440
And then that kind of shows you two things that like things aren't always as they seem.

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:20.060
And like the solution to the problem you're having is not always in the same room.

00:15:20.260 --> 00:15:22.540
You might have to go back and remember something else.

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:25.120
Like, I just feel like that sets up that for the game.

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:35.240
And an important thing here too, when we're talking about looking at guides or stuff like that, it's, this game is not a game that you can play with a guide.

00:15:35.480 --> 00:15:40.280
You're not playing it then at that point, especially because there's no inputs to be done really.

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:44.400
That feels like you're just, you know, watching a YouTube video at that point.

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:48.940
It feels weird doing everything in the order exactly as intended.

00:15:48.940 --> 00:15:59.320
This game is at its best when you have the, you know, as they reference it in the manual, the Shadowgate hint guide book, which you could have ordered for $5.99 back in the day.

00:16:00.020 --> 00:16:08.580
If that had come in the mail, which is what I wound up using for my playthrough, that just gives you hints in three different layers of difficulty.

00:16:08.580 --> 00:16:15.340
It has an A tier, a B tier, and a C for each kind of puzzle in the game.

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:17.740
So first you figure out. And C is just telling you where it is, right?

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:19.920
Right. C gives you the answer outright.

00:16:20.140 --> 00:16:22.080
B is a little more suggestive.

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:23.500
And A is so vague.

00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:25.280
It's like sometimes I just go right to B.

00:16:25.420 --> 00:16:26.880
I'm like, okay, well, what the hell does that mean?

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:31.080
Do these correspond to the in-game hints?

00:16:31.320 --> 00:16:33.380
No. No, they don't.

00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:40.140
It's more like there's a layout of very specific spots where you will get stuck in the game.

00:16:40.540 --> 00:16:44.100
And you look at that specific thing that you're stuck in.

00:16:44.100 --> 00:16:47.680
And it has a line in it for like, you know, what you're trying to do.

00:16:47.800 --> 00:16:52.520
And it says, go to A32, B47, or C6.

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:54.260
You know, so then you go to the index.

00:16:54.520 --> 00:16:55.840
First, you try the A thing.

00:16:55.980 --> 00:16:57.980
See if you can solve it based off of that.

00:16:57.980 --> 00:16:59.920
If you still can't figure it out, you go to the B.

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:05.480
And if you still can't figure it out, ideally, you would then go on to the C, which would tell you the answer outright.

00:17:05.840 --> 00:17:14.040
That's the creative way to still like get a solving mechanic that doesn't revolve around using a strategy guide for this game.

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:21.180
Because when you use a strategy guide, I don't know, the whole point of the game, right, is that you're going to die.

00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:28.080
They come up with so many kinds of creative deaths and weird deaths and things that you wouldn't think could kill you, do kill you.

00:17:28.760 --> 00:17:37.100
The point of the game is to explore and just kind of, you know, try to take every single thing in the game or open everything you see.

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:41.800
Because you're always just, when you die, you're just going to start back on the previous screen, right?

00:17:41.900 --> 00:17:44.940
So it's not like experimentation is frowned upon.

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:49.720
Using a strategy guide was something that right away just didn't sit right with me.

00:17:50.600 --> 00:17:51.460
Yeah, I agree.

00:17:51.620 --> 00:18:02.980
I feel like it's like if you were to compare it to like Super Mario Brothers and you're playing that and you're stuck in that endless dungeon and you just don't care about this puzzle because you just want to go stomp on some Goombas or whatever.

00:18:05.960 --> 00:18:09.680
Maybe you'd use a guide so you can get back to like the gameplay element that you like.

00:18:09.960 --> 00:18:12.720
This game, the gameplay element is figuring out where to go next.

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:19.840
So if you look up where to go next for everything, you're not doing the thing that is supposed to be the challenge.

00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:26.940
But I'm also, I'm not going to frown upon somebody that like has been trying to get past this one thing forever.

00:18:26.940 --> 00:18:29.160
And they've been playing normally up until then.

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:35.320
And they just, you know, they just need to get past it so that they can enjoy figuring out other things.

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:39.360
Yeah, and I won't pretend that I didn't use a guide at all either.

00:18:39.520 --> 00:18:41.540
I did a couple times towards the end.

00:18:41.860 --> 00:18:46.880
Only though, I'd say because, well, one, because I wanted to finish the game before we recorded this.

00:18:47.240 --> 00:18:56.980
And two, because I've just been burned by NES games before in the sense where it's like, well, this, you would absolutely never figure this out without a guide.

00:18:56.980 --> 00:19:01.480
So I'm like, well, if that's, what if that's happening right now and I'm just wasting my time and I need a guide.

00:19:01.720 --> 00:19:04.260
And then I would look and sometimes I would think that that's the case.

00:19:04.340 --> 00:19:07.780
And then maybe like later on, I would realize like, oh, you know what, there kind of was a hint.

00:19:07.900 --> 00:19:11.420
And if I had unlimited time, maybe eventually I would have figured this out.

00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:14.540
But like some of them are pretty, you know, pretty vague.

00:19:14.780 --> 00:19:19.940
So I like, I think they're all possible, but some of them would take you a long time.

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:29.540
Well, I had an experience like that earlyish on in the game where I got to the room with the tombs in it.

00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:34.720
There's like three tombs on each side and they're closed or whatever.

00:19:34.720 --> 00:19:37.120
So I just started opening them from left to right.

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:43.020
And I opened the middle one on the left and green slime oozed out.

00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:53.340
And it blocked me from exploring the rest of the tomb because no matter what I did, I would walk on that slime and it would be like, you just got slimed.

00:19:53.420 --> 00:19:55.360
And then the Grim Reaper would show up and I'm like, what?

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:56.520
Like that's a death?

00:19:57.080 --> 00:19:58.020
What's in the slime?

00:19:58.120 --> 00:19:59.700
Like, why can't I jump over it or whatever?

00:20:00.100 --> 00:20:01.520
Anyway, not the point.

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:09.660
The point being that for the longest time, I assumed that I had just like, I had just gotten stuck in the game.

